/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A multicomponent feed has components with different volatilities.
   - Non-key distribution is the amount of each non-key that leaves in distillate and in bottoms.
   - All shortcut methods assume constant relative volatility α and constant molar overflow (CMO). αi is the volatility of component i relative to the heavy key: αi = Ki/KHK, where Ki = yi/xi at equilibrium.
   - At total reflux no product is removed, so the column needs the fewest theoretical stages for a required separation.
   - Nmin is the minimum number of theoretical stages; dLK and dHK are molar flow rates of LK and HK in the distillate; bLK and bHK are molar flow rates of LK and HK in the bottoms; αLK-HK is the relative volatility of LK to HK, taken as a geome
   - Use molar flow rates, not mole fractions, in d and b.
   - Nmin is not the design stage count.
   - At minimum reflux the column has a pinch and needs infinite stages.
   - αi is the relative volatility of component i to the heavy key; zi is the feed mole fraction; q is the feed thermal condition (q = 1 for saturated liquid, q = 0 for saturated vapour); θ is a root lying between αLK and αHK.
   - Then use the same θ in the reflux equation.
*/
