/* book Worked example: Q: A saturated-liquid feed of 100 kmol/h contains benzene 0.40, toluene 0.35 and o-xylene 0.25 (mole fractions). Relative volatilities to toluene are 2.5, 1.0 and 0.45. Benzene is the light key and toluene the heavy key: recover 98 % of the benzene in the distillate and 98 % of the toluene in the bottoms. Find Nmin, the xylene split, Rmin by Underwood, and the stages at R = 1.3 Rmin by Gilliland.
   1. Key flows: dLK = 0.98 × 40 = 39.2 and bLK = 0.80 kmol/h; bHK = 0.98 × 35 = 34.3 and dHK = 0.70 kmol/h.
   2. Fenske: Nmin = log[(39.2/0.80)(34.3/0.70)]/log 2.5 = log(49 × 49)/log 2.5 = 3.3804/0.3979 = 8.49.
   3. Xylene: (d/b) = (0.70/34.3)(0.458.49) = 0.0204 × 0.00113 = 2.3 × 10⁻⁵, so practically all 25 kmol/h leave in the bottoms. D = 39.9 and B = 60.1 kmol/h.
   4. Underwood feed equation with q = 1: 2.5(0.40)/(2.5 − θ) + 1.0(0.35)/(1.0 − θ) + 0.45(0.25)/(0.45 − θ) = 0, with θ between 1.0 and 2.5. Trial gives θ = 1.428 (terms 0.933, −0.818, −0.115).
   5. Distillate mole fractions: benzene 39.2/39.9 = 0.9824, toluene 0.70/39.9 = 0.0175. Rmin + 1 = 2.5(0.9824)/(2.5 − 1.428) + 1.0(0.0175)/(1.0 − 1.428) = 2.291 − 0.041 = 2.250, so Rmin = 1.25.
   6. R = 1.3 × 1.250 = 1.625. X = (1.625 − 1.250)/(1.625 + 1) = 0.143. Molokanov: Y = 0.512.
   7. N = (8.49 + 0.512)/(1 − 0.512) = 18.4, so 19 theoretical stages including the reboiler.
   Answer:  */
