/* book exam traps: Underwood uses each component's αi. Fenske needs only αLK-HK, which appears in its denominator. | Nmin is the stage count at total reflux, not at the design reflux. Rmin needs infinite stages, not Nmin stages. | The Underwood θ must lie between αLK and αHK for the key root. A root outside this interval gives a meaningless Rmin. | q is the feed thermal condition, not the reflux ratio. Saturated liquid gives q = 1; saturated vapour gives q = 0. | d and b are molar flow rates, but D and B cancel inside the product (dLK/bLK)(bHK/dHK), so Fenske can always be written with mole fractions: Nmin = log10[(xD,LK xB,HK)/(xB,LK xD,HK)] / log10 αLK-HK. Just do not mix a flow in one factor with a mole fraction in  */
